## Phase 6 — Medication Intelligence & Drug Safety
**Duration:** 2–3 weeks

> [!WARNING]
> **Highest-risk subsystem. Clinical + regulatory sign-off required.** The LLM must NEVER calculate doses or name a medication from its own knowledge. It explains a result that the rules engine has already validated.

**Goal:** Implement the **Medication Safety Check** pipeline — deterministic rules-first, LLM explains only.

### Medication Safety Flow (from Medication Safety Check Sequence Diagram)
```
User: "Can I take this medicine?"
       ↓
POST /chat/turn
       ↓
Safety Engine pre-check → Safe/continue
       ↓
Orchestrator: medication query + tool contract
       ↓
Tool Gateway executes:
  get_current_medications() → retrieve active medications
  get_allergies()           → retrieve allergy profile
  search_drug_information() → query authoritative drug data
       ↓
Drug Knowledge Provider: drug facts + labeling data
       ↓
Clinical Rules Engine validates:
  - Contraindications (patient conditions vs drug)
  - Allergy check (patient allergies vs drug/excipients)
  - Drug-drug interactions (new drug vs current medications)
  - Age/sex/pregnancy checks (where applicable)
  - Renal/hepatic checks (where applicable)
  - Dosage/label validation
       ↓
Rules Engine returns: structured status + reasons + sources
       ↓
Medical LLM: explain validated result ONLY (cannot change or soften it)
       ↓
Response Validator: verify explanation matches rules result exactly
       ↓
[Escalation required] → Emergency/clinical escalation response
[Safe response]       → Validated explanation + sources → User
```

### What We're Building

**Clinical Rules Engine:**
- Deterministic medication validation (no LLM involvement in the decision)
- Checks: contraindication, allergy, drug-drug interaction, age/sex, renal/hepatic, dosage
- Returns structured result: `{status: SAFE|CAUTION|UNSAFE|ESCALATE, reasons: [...], sources: [...]}`

**Drug Knowledge Provider:**
- Integration with an authoritative drug database:
  - **FDA OpenFDA API** (free, US-focused)
  - **RxNorm** (drug normalization)
  - **DrugBank** (interactions, licensed)
  - **OpenFDA Drug Labels** (prescribing info)
- Do NOT derive interaction data from general model knowledge

**Medication Intelligence API:**
```
POST   /medication/check            – Check if medication is safe for patient
GET    /drugs/{rxnorm_id}/info      – Drug facts + labeling
GET    /drugs/{rxnorm_id}/interactions?with=drug_a,drug_b – Interaction check
```

### Technology Stack
| Component | Technology |
|---|---|
| Drug Database | FDA OpenFDA REST API + DrugBank (licensed) |
| Drug Normalization | RxNorm REST API (HTTP client via `axios`) |
| Rules Engine | Node.js (TypeScript) — deterministic rule functions, zero LLM involvement |

### Deliverables
- [ ] Drug Knowledge Provider integrated with authoritative source
- [ ] Clinical Rules Engine performing all 6 check types
- [ ] Rules Engine result is never modified by LLM
- [ ] Response Validator verifies explanation consistency with rules result
- [ ] Escalation flow when rules return ESCALATE status
- [ ] Clinical + regulatory sign-off on all rule definitions and thresholds
- [ ] Integration test: known contraindication → correctly returns UNSAFE + escalation

### Acceptance Criteria
- Aspirin for a patient with salicylate allergy → returns UNSAFE with allergy reason
- Known drug-drug interaction pair → returns CAUTION or UNSAFE with interaction source
- LLM explanation matches rules result exactly (validator catches any deviation)
- Patient-specific dosage calculation is never generated by the LLM (test with prompts)
- All medication decisions are logged with: drug name, patient context used, rules result, sources

